    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<p>Broad beans vs kidney beans. They are common legumes in our daily lives. The major suppliers of these beans worldwide are mainly located in countries such as Australia, France, the UK, Ethiopia, Canada, Mexico, and Argentina. In China, regions like Jiangsu, Anhui, and Hubei provide a stable supply of broad beans. Additionally, areas with higher altitudes, such as Gansu and Qinghai, are also major producers of broad beans. On the other hand, Hebei, Shanxi, Inner Mongolia, Jilin, and Heilongjiang are the main regions for kidney bean production. Notably, China is the world&#8217;s third-largest producer of kidney beans.</p><p><img src="/images/1728894637310-300x199.jpg" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-480" alt="Broad beans vs kidney beans" width="525" height="348" title="Broad beans vs kidney beans | The Most Useful Knowledge 4" /></p><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_85 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#What_are_the_major_production_areas_for_broad_beans_and_kidney_beans_both_globally_and_in_China" >What are the major production areas for broad beans and kidney beans, both globally and in China?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#What_are_the_planting_methods_for_broad_beans_and_kidney_beans" >What are the planting methods for broad beans and kidney beans?</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#Broad_Beans" >Broad Beans:</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#Kidney_Beans" >Kidney Beans:</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#What_are_their_respective_yields" >What are their respective yields?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#Which_is_more_nutritious" >Which is more nutritious?</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#Broad_Beans-2" >Broad Beans:</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="#Kidney_Beans-2" >Kidney Beans:</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="#What_are_the_varieties_of_kidney_beans" >What are the varieties of kidney beans?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-10" href="#What_are_the_characteristics_of_traditional_cooking_in_China_and_abroad" >What are the characteristics of traditional cooking in China and abroad?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-11" href="#How_to_Choose_the_Best_Fava_Beans_and_Kidney_Beans" >How to Choose the Best Fava Beans and Kidney Beans?</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-12" href="#Fava_Beans" >Fava Beans:</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-13" href="#Kidney_Beans-3" >Kidney Beans:</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-14" href="#What_Are_the_Prices_of_Fava_Beans_and_Kidney_Beans" >What Are the Prices of Fava Beans and Kidney Beans?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-15" href="#How_to_Store_Fava_Beans_and_Kidney_Beans" >How to Store Fava Beans and Kidney Beans?</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_are_the_major_production_areas_for_broad_beans_and_kidney_beans_both_globally_and_in_China"></span>What are the major production areas for broad beans and kidney beans, both globally and in China?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The main production areas for broad beans and kidney beans are as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Broad Beans:</strong> Broad beans originated in Southwest Asia and North Africa. Today, they are cultivated and distributed worldwide, primarily in Europe, Asia, and Africa. China is the largest producer of broad beans globally.</li><li><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong> Originally from Central and South America, kidney beans later spread to Europe and Asia, becoming a global crop. Major producing countries include China, the United States, Argentina, Mexico, the UK, Japan, India, and Brazil. In China, kidney beans are widely grown across various regions.</li></ul><p>Now, let’s look at their main production areas within China:</p><ul><li><strong>Broad Beans:</strong> Primarily grown in the southwest, the Yangtze River basin, and the northwest regions, including Yunnan, Sichuan, Chongqing, Hubei, Gansu, and Qinghai.</li><li><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong> Primarily distributed in the northeast, north, northwest, and southwest regions, such as Heilongjiang, Jilin, Inner Mongolia, Hebei, Shanxi, Shaanxi, Gansu, Xinjiang, Sichuan, Guizhou, and Yunnan.</li></ul><p>These regions have suitable climates and soil conditions, making them the major production areas for broad beans and kidney beans.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_are_the_planting_methods_for_broad_beans_and_kidney_beans"></span>What are the planting methods for broad beans and kidney beans?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>After understanding their production areas, let&#8217;s learn about their planting methods.</p><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Broad_Beans"></span><strong>Broad Beans:</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul><li><strong>Soil Selection:</strong> Choose loose, fertile, and well-drained soil.</li><li><strong>Seed Treatment:</strong> Soak the seeds for 12 to 24 hours before sowing to improve germination rates.</li><li><strong>Sowing Method:</strong> Direct sowing or transplanting. Direct sowing involves scattering seeds directly in the field, while transplanting involves growing seedlings in nursery boxes and then transplanting them into the field.</li><li><strong>Management Practices:</strong> Keep the soil moist, weed in time, apply sufficient base fertilizer, provide timely top-dressing, and control pests and diseases.</li></ul><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Kidney_Beans"></span><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul><li><strong>Soil Selection:</strong> Fertile soil with good aeration and deep soil layers, with convenient irrigation and drainage.</li><li><strong>Seed Treatment:</strong> Select large, plump, uniform seeds with consistent luster, free of pests and damage. Dry and soak the seeds before planting, followed by chemical treatment.</li><li><strong>Sowing Method:</strong> Either monocropping or intercropping. Monocropping requires ridging, with appropriate row and plant spacing. Intercropping involves planting alongside other crops, and adjusting planting density according to the characteristics of the companion crop.</li><li><strong>Management Practices:</strong> Keep the soil moist, weed, and ridge in time, stake the plants, plant at an appropriate density, apply timely top-dressing, and control pests and diseases.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_are_their_respective_yields"></span>What are their respective yields?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><ul><li><strong>Broad Beans:</strong> Under normal planting conditions, the yield of fresh broad beans is generally around 150 to 200 kg per mu (approximately 0.067 hectares), with high-yield instances reaching up to 280 kg per mu. For example, in Qujing City, high-yield demonstration plots can average between 250 and 290 kg per mu.</li><li><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong> When grown in sunny, warm environments, the yield can reach approximately 2,650 kg per mu. In Zhi Jin County’s high-yield demonstration base, the average yield ranges from 133.42 to 148.65 kg per mu, with the highest recorded yield at 163.97 kg.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Which_is_more_nutritious"></span>Which is more nutritious?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>To determine which bean is more nutritious, let’s look at their specific nutritional components.</p><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Broad_Beans-2"></span><strong>Broad Beans:</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamins:</strong> Rich in various vitamins, including vitamins A, K, C, E, B1 (thiamine), B2 (riboflavin), and niacin. The vitamin C content is particularly high, with about 16 to 30 mg per 100 grams of broad beans.</li><li><strong>Minerals:</strong> High in calcium, phosphorus, potassium, sodium, magnesium, iron, zinc, selenium, copper, and manganese. Phosphorus and potassium levels are notably high, with approximately 200 mg of phosphorus and 391 to 801 mg of potassium per 100 grams.</li><li><strong>Fat:</strong> Relatively low fat content, with about 1.00 to 1.6 grams per 100 grams.</li><li><strong>Dietary Fiber:</strong> Contains a good amount of dietary fiber, aiding in intestinal health, with about 1.9 to 3.1 grams per 100 grams.</li><li><strong>Sugars:</strong> Higher sugar content, typically around 61.5 grams per 100 grams, primarily in the form of carbohydrates (58.9 to 59.8 grams per 100 grams).</li></ul><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Kidney_Beans-2"></span><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul><li><strong>Vitamins:</strong> Rich in vitamins, especially B vitamins and vitamin C, which is about 9 mg per 100 grams. It also contains vitamins A, E, B1, B2, and niacin.</li><li><strong>Minerals:</strong> Contains significant amounts of minerals, particularly calcium, iron, and phosphorus. Calcium content ranges from 88 to 349 mg per 100 grams, phosphorus from 37 to 400 mg, and iron from 1 to 2.8 mg. Also contains zinc and magnesium.</li><li><strong>Fat:</strong> Low fat content, approximately 0.56 to 1.4 grams per 100 grams.</li><li><strong>Dietary Fiber:</strong> High in dietary fiber, promoting gastrointestinal health, with about 7.4 grams per 100 grams.</li><li><strong>Sugars:</strong> Moderate sugar content, primarily in carbohydrate form, around 22 to 57.4 grams per 100 grams, with lower sugar levels of about 0.3 to 6.4 grams (depending on variety and ripeness).</li></ul><p>Both beans have distinct nutritional advantages, making it difficult to declare one as definitively better than the other; it largely depends on individual dietary needs.</p><p>For instance, kidney beans are known for aiding digestion, weight loss, blood enrichment, spleen health, lowering blood sugar, and protecting blood vessels. Broad beans benefit brain and nerve health, enhancing memory and cognitive function. Additionally, broad beans are low in calories, and suitable for individuals with high cholesterol or hypertension. They also have effects on energy boosting, spleen health, and reducing edema.</p><p>Ultimately, the choice between kidney beans and broad beans can be based on personal nutritional needs and health conditions.</p><p><img src="/images/1728894762751-300x237.jpg" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-481" alt="Broad beans vs kidney beans" width="681" height="538" title="Broad beans vs kidney beans | The Most Useful Knowledge 5" /></p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_are_the_varieties_of_kidney_beans"></span>What are the varieties of kidney beans?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Kidney beans come in many varieties. Here are some of the notable types:</p><table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; border: 1px solid black;"><thead><tr><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Variety</th><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Color</th><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Texture</th><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Taste</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">White Kidney Bean</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Usually white or light yellow</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Delicate</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Sweet and crunchy, tender</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Red Kidney Bean</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Red or purple-red</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Plump</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Delicious with a chewy texture</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Milk Flower Kidney Bean</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Typically milky white or light yellow with milk flower spots</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Firm</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Sweet with a unique milk flower flavor</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Small Black Kidney Bean</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Black or dark purple</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Hard</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Delicious with a certain toughness</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Large White Kidney Bean</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">White or light yellow, larger than white kidney beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Soft and plump</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Sweet and suitable for various dishes</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_are_the_characteristics_of_traditional_cooking_in_China_and_abroad"></span>What are the characteristics of traditional cooking in China and abroad?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Let&#8217;s explore the culinary characteristics of fava beans and kidney beans in China, India, and Italy:</p><table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; border: 1px solid black;"><thead><tr><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Variety</th><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Country</th><th style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Cooking Characteristics</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Fava Beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">China</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Fava beans are cooked in various ways, including stir-frying, boiling, steaming, roasting, and frying. Popular dishes include scallion oil fava beans, five-spice fava beans, fava beans stir-fried with shredded meat, and fava beans stir-fried with shrimp. The spring delicacy &#8220;scallion oil fava beans&#8221; exudes a unique fragrance of spring.</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Fava Beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">India</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">In Indian cooking, fava beans are often combined with various spices and seasonings, imparting a rich Indian flavor to the beans.</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Fava Beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Italy</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Italians enjoy eating fava beans raw after shelling them. They also make a fava bean and garlic pesto (Pesto Di Fave) to pair with bread or turn the beans into a puree served with pasta. Additionally, they may cook fava beans in broth with quality cured meats, parsley, and mint for seasoning.</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Kidney Beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">China</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">In southern China, kidney beans are often incorporated into light vegetarian dishes, such as cold tossed kidney beans or stir-fried kidney beans, emphasizing the beans&#8217; crisp texture. In northern China, kidney beans are more commonly used in hearty dishes, reflecting the northern preference for robust flavors.</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Kidney Beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">India</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Kidney beans are a primary ingredient in dishes like Dal Makhani (buttery lentils), which are made with black lentils and red kidney beans cooked with cream or yogurt, making it a popular North Indian dish. Rajma, a stew made with red kidney beans and various spices, is often served during celebrations and special occasions.</td></tr><tr><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Kidney Beans</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">Italy</td><td style="border: 1px solid black; padding: 8px;">White kidney beans are often the main ingredient in hearty vegetable soups, simmered with salted pork and cheese for a rich and aromatic broth.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="How_to_Choose_the_Best_Fava_Beans_and_Kidney_Beans"></span><strong>How to Choose the Best Fava Beans and Kidney Beans?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Having gained a comprehensive understanding of fava beans and kidney beans, I would like to share how we should select the best fava beans and kidney beans. We can choose based on their color, shape, and texture.</p><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Fava_Beans"></span><strong>Fava Beans:</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul><li><strong>Color:</strong> Fava beans should be a bright green color, with the brighter, the better. If the fava beans come with pods, the pod skin and the sides should also be green. At the same time, avoid purchasing artificially dyed fava beans; you can blow gently on the fava beans and lightly squeeze them to see if the color comes off.</li><li><strong>Shape:</strong> Fava beans should be flat and round; choose full and round fava beans, as these are fresher and have better taste. Avoid selecting shriveled or incomplete fava beans.</li><li><strong>Texture:</strong> Good fava beans should be hard and not easily squished. If the fava beans feel soft when squeezed, it indicates they are no longer fresh or of poor quality, which will negatively affect their taste.</li></ul><h3><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Kidney_Beans-3"></span><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul><li><strong>Color:</strong> High-quality kidney beans are usually bright and uniform in color; for instance, white kidney beans should be pure white, and red kidney beans should be bright red. Avoid choosing kidney beans that are dull in color or have spots. The surface of kidney beans should have a certain natural luster, indicating that they are fresh and well-preserved. If the surface of the kidney beans is dull, it may indicate they have been stored for a while.</li><li><strong>Shape:</strong> High-quality kidney beans should be plump and rounded without obvious dents or deformities. Such kidney beans are more likely to maintain their integrity when cooked, resulting in better taste. Choosing evenly sized kidney beans ensures uniform cooking, avoiding cases where some kidney beans are overcooked while others are undercooked.</li><li><strong>Texture:</strong> Gently squeeze the kidney beans; if they feel firm and are not easily crushed, it indicates they are fresh and of good quality. If the kidney beans feel soft or have a hollow sensation, it may indicate they have deteriorated or been stored for too long. While it is not possible to directly assess the cooking taste through appearance, generally, high-quality kidney beans will retain a certain chewiness and delicious flavor after cooking. If the kidney beans become overly mushy or taste poor after cooking, it may suggest poor quality or improper cooking methods.</li></ul><p>In summary, by carefully observing the color and shape of the kidney beans and gently squeezing them to feel their texture, you can select high-quality fava beans and kidney beans.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_Are_the_Prices_of_Fava_Beans_and_Kidney_Beans"></span><strong>What Are the Prices of Fava Beans and Kidney Beans?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The raw material prices for fava beans and kidney beans are as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Fava Beans:</strong> There are price differences based on different regions and markets. In Fujian and Yunnan, the wholesale price is about 8 yuan per kilogram; at Beijing&#8217;s Dayang Road Agricultural and Sideline Products Wholesale Market, the price is about 11-12 yuan per kilogram; in Wuhan&#8217;s Baishazhou Agricultural and Sideline Products Market, the price is about 10 yuan per kilogram.</li><li><strong>Kidney Beans:</strong> The recent price is 15.5 yuan per kilogram.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="How_to_Store_Fava_Beans_and_Kidney_Beans"></span><strong>How to Store Fava Beans and Kidney Beans?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Let&#8217;s discuss how to store fava beans and kidney beans from four aspects: temperature, humidity, environment, and packaging.</p><ul><li><strong>Temperature:</strong> The storage temperature for fava beans is generally kept between 5℃ and 10℃; for kidney beans, the storage temperature should be between 5℃ and 8℃.</li><li><strong>Humidity:</strong> The storage humidity for fava beans should be controlled between 10% and 35%, with an average humidity of 15%-20% being optimal; the relative humidity for kidney beans should be 65%-75%.</li><li><strong>Environment:</strong> Both fava beans and kidney beans should be stored in a cool, dark, dry, and well-ventilated place, with effective measures to prevent moisture, insects, and rodents. Fava beans should be kept out of direct sunlight to reduce oxidation and discoloration, while kidney beans should be kept away from toxic, harmful, or damp items during storage.</li><li><strong>Packaging:</strong> When storing fava beans and kidney beans, they can be packaged in plastic bags or vacuum bags to enhance sealing and prevent moisture and damage. The packaging should ensure that the fava beans and kidney beans are completely dry.</li></ul><p>Additionally, regularly check the stored fava beans and kidney beans, and promptly remove any moldy or spoiled beans to ensure effective storage.</p>`} />
